15
00:00:42,02 --> 00:00:43,06
We're going to look at the source code.

16
00:00:43,06 --> 00:00:46,06
And you can see that now we are using the React API

17
00:00:46,06 --> 00:00:48,07
in order to manage the state.

18
00:00:48,07 --> 00:00:51,08
And the concern here is that the state remains attached

19
00:00:51,08 --> 00:00:54,06
to the same components.

20
00:00:54,06 --> 00:00:56,07
And this can become quickly an issue

21
00:00:56,07 --> 00:00:58,05
if you need to scale the up,

22
00:00:58,05 --> 00:01:00,09
or maintaining the state can be a very tedious

23
00:01:00,09 --> 00:01:02,09
and challenging task.

24
00:01:02,09 --> 00:01:03,09
In previous examples,

25
00:01:03,09 --> 00:01:05,06
we have seen that the application state

26
00:01:05,06 --> 00:01:08,03
should never remain attached to a single components

27
00:01:08,03 --> 00:01:11,03
or remain at the top level of the application tree.

28
00:01:11,03 --> 00:01:13,08
Hence, the need to centralize the state management

29
00:01:13,08 --> 00:01:16,00
and to keep the structure flat.

30
00:01:16,00 --> 00:01:20,01
So that's why we're going to use zustand in this example.

31
00:01:20,01 --> 00:01:22,09
And to get started, we're going to first create a store.

32
00:01:22,09 --> 00:01:24,02
So let's go back here.

33
00:01:24,02 --> 00:01:26,02
We're going to look at the documentation.

34
00:01:26,02 --> 00:01:29,06
And first you need to import the function create

35
00:01:29,06 --> 00:01:31,08
from the library zustand,

36
00:01:31,08 --> 00:01:34,06
and we're going to be able to create a store,

37
00:01:34,06 --> 00:01:35,08
which will be a hook.

38
00:01:35,08 --> 00:01:39,08
So let's copy from this example.

39
00:01:39,08 --> 00:01:42,09
Let's go back to our source code and the project,

40
00:01:42,09 --> 00:01:43,08
the starter project.

41
00:01:43,08 --> 00:01:50,06
And inside here we're going to go ahead and create a store.

42
00:01:50,06 --> 00:01:52,08
And I'm going to paste those lines.

43
00:01:52,08 --> 00:01:57,02
And the way it works is that the store is like a big hook

44
00:01:57,02 --> 00:02:00,07
that can include anything, objects to all the states,

45
00:02:00,07 --> 00:02:04,08
primitive data, and also functions to carry over the states.

46
00:02:04,08 --> 00:02:07,08
So we're going to invoke the create functions from the library,

47
00:02:07,08 --> 00:02:10,03
which is already in the scope.

48
00:02:10,03 --> 00:02:14,01
And I'm going to name this hook useCounter.

49
00:02:14,01 --> 00:02:18,05
For the initial state, it's going to be count that we set at 0.

50
00:02:18,05 --> 00:02:22,01
And we're going to have three features to implement,

51
00:02:22,01 --> 00:02:24,03
which is increment, decrement, and reset.

52
00:02:24,03 --> 00:02:26,05
So let's set this up.

53
00:02:26,05 --> 00:02:29,06
It's going to be increment.

54
00:02:29,06 --> 00:02:33,06
And for now, I'm just going to leave this as an empty function.

55
00:02:33,06 --> 00:02:39,05
Then it's going to be decrement.

56
00:02:39,05 --> 00:02:45,09
Finally, reset, to reset the counter at 0.

57
00:02:45,09 --> 00:02:48,00
And so the create function takes a callback function

58
00:02:48,00 --> 00:02:49,07
that would hold the store data

59
00:02:49,07 --> 00:02:53,00
with global states and actions, just like we did right here.

60
00:02:53,00 --> 00:02:54,02
And what we're going to do right away

61
00:02:54,02 --> 00:02:58,00
is to export default this useCounter hook

62
00:02:58,00 --> 00:03:00,09
because we're going to need to use it outside.

63
00:03:00,09 --> 00:03:04,01
And the store callback exposes a set function

64
00:03:04,01 --> 00:03:05,07
and also a get function.

65
00:03:05,07 --> 00:03:08,00
So we're going to use set to update the state,

66
00:03:08,00 --> 00:03:10,07
but also get to read the state.

67
00:03:10,07 --> 00:03:13,09
So the first thing we want to do is to read from the state.

68
00:03:13,09 --> 00:03:17,03
We're going to need to have access to the count.

69
00:03:17,03 --> 00:03:19,08
And just to show that this is private,

70
00:03:19,08 --> 00:03:23,05
I'm going to do get and then count.

71
00:03:23,05 --> 00:03:25,08
And after that, we're going to use set

72
00:03:25,08 --> 00:03:28,05
in order to update the states.

73
00:03:28,05 --> 00:03:33,05
So that's going to be this syntax.

74
00:03:33,05 --> 00:03:36,09
And actually what it does is that it returns in object

75
00:03:36,09 --> 00:03:38,01
that's going to be wrapped here

76
00:03:38,01 --> 00:03:40,09
in another parenthesis, like this.

77
00:03:40,09 --> 00:03:43,02
And then we're going to update the counts.

78
00:03:43,02 --> 00:03:45,02
And actually let's just save some lines of code.

79
00:03:45,02 --> 00:03:48,08
And we're going to do get counts + 1 to increments.

80
00:03:48,08 --> 00:03:51,03
We don't need this line right here.

81
00:03:51,03 --> 00:03:52,09
I'm going to do the same for decrement,

82
00:03:52,09 --> 00:03:59,09
but actually it's going to be - 1, and same for resets.

83
00:03:59,09 --> 00:04:04,06
And here we're going to reset the value at 0.

84
00:04:04,06 --> 00:04:06,02
And I think that works.

85
00:04:06,02 --> 00:04:08,04
So creating the store was an easy step.

86
00:04:08,04 --> 00:04:11,03
The syntax is very similar to the context API,

87
00:04:11,03 --> 00:04:13,05
but the only difference is that it doesn't require

88
00:04:13,05 --> 00:04:16,06
the app components to be wrapped in a context provider.

89
00:04:16,06 --> 00:04:18,06
So the data flow is very simple.

90
00:04:18,06 --> 00:04:19,04
And next we're going to see

91
00:04:19,04 --> 00:04:22,00
how to bind the components to the states.

